  "observations": {
    "whatHappened": "Universal indicator turned red in the acid, green in water and blue-purple in the alkali. Litmus turned red in the acid and blue in the alkali.",
    "why": "Indicators are weak acids whose two forms have different colours. Lots of H⁺ (acid) pushes them into one form; lots of OH⁻ (alkali) into the other. Universal indicator mixes several dyes, so it shows a whole rainbow across pH 1 to 14.",
    "equationLatex": "\\mathrm{HIn \\rightleftharpoons H^+ + In^-}",
    "realWorld": [
      "Soil testing for gardening",
      "Swimming-pool chemistry",
      "Red cabbage as a kitchen indicator"
    ],
